## Multi-Tenancy

Every YOM MongoDB query in NestJS services **must be scoped to `customerId`**. This is a hard rule — unscoped queries are a data isolation bug.

### Rule
- Every repository method that reads or writes data must accept and apply a `customerId` filter
- The `customerId` comes from the authenticated request context (decoded JWT) and is passed down through the use case to the repository
- Never call `find()`, `findOne()`, `findById()`, `findByIdAndUpdate()`, etc. without including `{ customerId }` in the filter

### Correct vs Wrong

```typescript
// CORRECT — always scope queries to customerId
async findAll(customerId: string): Promise<Banner[]> {
  const docs = await this.model
    .find({ customerId })
    .lean()
    .exec();
  return docs.map(BannerMapper.toDomain);
}

async findById(id: string, customerId: string): Promise<Banner | null> {
  const doc = await this.model
    .findOne({ _id: id, customerId })
    .lean()
    .exec();
  return doc ? BannerMapper.toDomain(doc) : null;
}

// WRONG — missing customerId scope, leaks data across tenants
async findAll(): Promise<Banner[]> {
  return this.model.find().lean().exec(); // never do this
}

async findById(id: string): Promise<Banner | null> {
  return this.model.findById(id).lean().exec(); // never do this
}
```

### Schema Design
```typescript
@Schema({ timestamps: true, collection: 'banners' })
export class BannerDocument {
  @Prop({ required: true, index: true })
  customerId: string; // always present, always indexed

  @Prop({ required: true })
  title: string;

  @Prop({ required: true, enum: ['active', 'inactive'] })
  status: string;
}

export const BannerSchema = SchemaFactory.createForClass(BannerDocument);
BannerSchema.index({ customerId: 1, createdAt: -1 });
```

